This will be my first Journal in regards to DOET - Chapter 1

One of the ideas from Chapter 1 that interested me most was Norman’s discussion of discoverability and understanding. He explains that good design should help people recognize what actions are possible and understand how an object is supposed to work (Norman, p. 3). I liked this idea because it changes the way I think about situations where I have trouble using something. Normally, it is easy to assume that I simply do not understand the object, but Norman argues that sometimes the design itself is failing to communicate with the user. His example involving confusing doors makes this especially clear. A door should be an extremely simple object, yet people can still hesitate because they cannot tell whether they are supposed to push, pull, or slide it.

I can relate this idea to everyday technology and appliances. For example, there have been times when I have looked at a set of controls and had to experiment with several buttons before figuring out which one actually did what I wanted. I usually blamed myself for not understanding it immediately. After reading this chapter, I agree with Norman that if many people have the same problem, then the designer should probably reconsider how clearly the controls communicate their purpose. Something should not require unnecessary trial and error when its basic function could have been made obvious.

Another topic I found interesting was human-centered design. Norman explains that human-centered design begins by considering people's actual needs, capabilities, and behaviors instead of designing around an ideal version of how people are supposed to behave (Norman, pp. 8–9). I strongly agree with this because people naturally make mistakes. A designer cannot simply assume that every user will perfectly understand instructions or always choose the correct control. Norman even points out that experts make errors, meaning a good product should anticipate mistakes instead of punishing users for them.

This also connects to Norman's discussion of feedback. He explains that feedback lets people know that their action has actually been recognized by the system (Norman, p. 23). I immediately thought about elevator buttons. When I press a button and it lights up, I know the elevator received my request. Without that response, I would probably press it again because I would not know whether anything happened. It is a very small design feature, but it greatly changes how confident a person feels while using something.
